## KEYWORDS
metastable alloys, metal anode, potassium-ion battery, metal-organic frameworks (MOF)-derived materials
## Introduction
Potassium-ion batteries (PIBs) are considered as a promising alternative to lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) owing to the abundant potassium resources, low costs, and fast ionic conductivity in electrolyte [l-3]. To achieve full PIBs with good stability, fabrication of anode materials that can store potassium over a low potential range (around $0.3\mathrm{V}$ ）to avoid dendrite formation is an important key [4]. While metals in groups 13-15 of the periodic table do meet these requirements [5,6],their severe pulverization often leads to loss of electrical contact during K-ion uptake/extraction [6-8]. This problem has been addressed by using intermetallic compounds of these metallic elements as anodes [9,10]. It was observed that the intermetallic compounds would be decomposed into their constituting metal upon discharging. The two metallic phases can better accommodate the volume changes and/or form better electrical conducting paths [1l,12]. Indeed, improved cycling stability was observed in intermetallic anodes. However, due to the strong intermetallic bonds, the separated metals re-alloy and form intermetallic compounds again upon charging [l2-l5]. For this reason, the issue of pulverization in long-term tests is not fully addressed.
Based on these pioneering works, it is natural to ask a question that, if we can stop the re-alloying process in the intermetallics upon charging, will the issue of pulverization be much better addressed? Here, we attempt to answer this question by preparing metastable alloys anodes which can naturally stop the re-alloying process upon charging because of their metastable nature. The main challenge is that due to the metastable nature, it is difficult to obtain such nanoscaled alloy materials [l6-l8]. In fact, up till now, there is no report on metastable alloy anodes for PIBs.
In this work, we prepare metastable Bi:Co and Bi:Fe alloy anodes for the first time from their metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) precursors. In the MOF's microenvironment, the two types of metal ions are distributed homogenously. Upon annealing decomposition treatment, they form metastable alloy, instead of elemental metallic, nanoparticles. These alloy nanodots are embedded in amorphous carbon stemming from the organic constituents in the MOFs. To demonstrate the electrochemical superiority of the metastable alloys, Bi is chosen as the active alloying element due to its high theoretical capacity [19-23]. Similar to the reported stable intermetallic anodes, the present metastable alloy anodes will also be decomposed into their constituting metal elements upon potassiation. However, due to their metastable nature, the alloys cannot be reformed upon depotassiation. Ex situ studies on Bi:Co@C show that the precipitated elemental active (Bi） and inactive (Co） nanoparticles are of $10{-}20~\mathrm{~nm}$ diameter and distribute uniformly. The electrochemically inactive Co nanodots can act as (1)“conductive binders” as well as (2）“separators” to prevent the severe aggregation of adjacent Bi precipitates and (3) accelerate the potassiation/de-potassiation kinetics in active Bi precipitates during the discharge/charge cycles. With these effects, the metastable alloy anodes deliver high rate K-ion storage under high current density of $20\ \mathrm{\A{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ (178 and $253\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ for the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes respectively） with stable cycling performance at $2\ {\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ . The capacity and cycling stability of the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ anode are among the best comparing to recently reported PIB anode materials.
## 2 Results and discussion
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) measurement was employed to analyze microstructures of the $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{:Co@C}}$ particles. It is observed that the synthesized sample composes of loosely packed nanodots embedded in carbon (Figs. 1(d) and 1(e)). High magnification TEM images show that the diameter of sphere-like nanodots is below $\sim20\:\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S3(a) in the ESM) and the coated carbon is of amorphous nature (Fig. S3(b) in the ESM). Elemental mapping results (Figs. S3(c)-S3(e) in the ESM) confirm the nanodots are composed of Bi and Co meanwhile the nanodots are uniformly distributed in the carbon matrix. To get more information about the nanodots, high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) studies were conducted. As shown in Fig.l(f),a metal particle shows lattice fringe intervals of 0.339 and $0.201\ \mathrm{nm}$ with angle of $119.9^{\circ}$ .While, these values do not match with those of crystalline cobalt, they are similar to the lattice spacing of (0l2) and (006) planes (corresponding to 0.328 and $0.197~\mathrm{nm}$ ，respectively）with an angle of $122.7^{\circ}$ of rhombohedral Bi. Another metal particle demonstrates lattice fringe intervals of 0.208 and $0.231~\mathrm{nm}$ with angle of $118.5^{\circ}(\mathrm{Fig.1(g)})$ . These values are also similar to the crystalline parameters of rhombohedral Bi (intervals of 0.202 and 0.227 for (015) and (ll0） planes, respectively, angle of $116.4^{\circ})$ · These HRTEM results are consistent with the XRD patterns. During the annealing process, cobalt with small atomic radius can dissolved into the lattice of rhombohedral Bi particles to form metastable Bi:Co alloy particles. It is believed that the formation of alloy instead of elemental metallic nanodots is due to the microenvironment in the MOFs in which the two types of metal cations are homogenously distributed [l6]. Physical examinations indicate that the Bi-Fe MOF-derived material also demonstrate metastable Bi:Fe alloy nanodots@carbon structure $(\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@{\mathrm C},$ Fig.S4 in the ESM). Diameter of the Bi:Fe alloy nanodots is also below $\sim\ 20~\mathrm{\nm}$ ： The Bi MOF-derived material shows Bi nanodots@carbon structure $(\mathrm{Bi}@{\mathrm{C}},$ Fig. S5 in the ESM).
Electrochemical performances of the $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}@\mathrm{C}$ and the Bi:Fe $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{Q}}\mathbf{C}$ materials were evaluated in 1 M KPF6/dimethoxyethane (DME) solutions over a potential window of $0.1\mathrm{-}1.5\mathrm{~V~}$ in 2032 coin cells with K foils as the counter electrodes. After activation of the initial discharge process, the $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes demonstrate a typical Bi-based K-ion uptake/release profiles (Figs. S6(a) and S6(b) in the
ESM) [19-23], implying the main energy storage might be mainly contributed by elemental Bi nanodots precipitated from the Bi:Co alloys. Charge/discharge tests under different current densities show superior specific capacity and rate capability for the $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}\ @\mathrm{C}$ electrodes. As demonstrated in Fig.2(a), along with the increase of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ molar ratio, rate capability of the $\operatorname{Bic}\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes first increases and then decreases with the best performance in the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode. The $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode delivers a specific discharge capacity of 364 and $258\ \mathrm{mAh{g}^{-1}}$ at 0.2 and $5\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ，which is considerably higher than those of the $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode (301 and $217\ \mathrm{mAh{g}^{-1}}$ 。respectively). Notably,the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.73}\mathrm{Co}_{0.27}@{\mathrm C}$ electrodes respectively demonstrate high capacity of 178 and $152\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ at a high current density of $20\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in contrast to the much lower capacity of $77\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode. The $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes with areal mass loadings between 0.7 to $11.2\ \mathrm{mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ exhibit a similar rate capability, confirming the electrochemical advantages of the $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}@\mathrm{C}$ materials (Fig.2(b)).
The $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode delivers superior rate capability of 354,279, and $253\mathrm{mAh\cdotg^{-1}}$ at 0.2, 5, and $20\mathrm{A\cdotg^{-1}};$ respectively (Fig. $2(\mathrm{c})$ and Figs. S7(a) and ${\mathsf{S}}7({\mathsf{b}})$ in the ESM). Capacity retention of the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ and the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ anodes under such high current densities shows performance comparable to state-of-theart metallic, sulfide, carbon, and oxide anodes (Fig.2(d)) [20,22,25-34], indicating their potential for practical PIB applications. The $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}\ @\mathrm{C}$ and the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe@C}$ materials  also demonstrate significantly enhanced cycling stability. As shown in Fig.2(e) and Figs. S7(c) in the ESM, they demonstrate stable cycling performance for approximately 1,ooo cycles at a high current density of $2\ \mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The maintained discharge capacities of the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ and the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes are 218 and
$279\mathrm{\mAh{g}^{-1}}$ after l,000 and 975 cycles, respectively. The mass loading-independent cycling performance of $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ further confirms the electrochemical advantages of the metastable alloy $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}$ materials prepared by the proposed approach (Fig. S6(c) in the ESM).
K-ion uptake mechanism of the Bi:Co@C electrodes was first investigated through ex situ XRD studies (Fig. 3(a)). $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ material (mixed with $5\ \mathrm{\textrm{wt}\%}$ of super $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ and $5\ \mathrm{~wt.\%~}$ of polyvinylidene fluoride) was casted on a titanium foil. As demonstrated in Figs. 3(b) and 3(c), accompanying with the increase of capacity, the typical Bi-K alloying-based storage characteristic becomes obvious during the initial four cycles. These phenomena indicate that there is an electrochemical activation process occurring in the prepared metastable Bi:Co nanodots. As shown by ex situ XRD patterns (Fig.3(a)), upon initial potassiation to $0.1~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ 、XRD signals of the Bi:Co alloy decrease, meanwhile the signals assigned to $\mathrm{K_{3}B i}$ and elemental fcc Co precipitate increase. The fcc Co XRD peak does not disappear when electrode is charged to $1.5\mathrm{V};$ indicating the prepared Bi:Co alloy does not recover. During the following discharge/charge cycles, along with the reversible phase changes between Bi and ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}{\mathrm{Bi}}_{:}$ fcc Co always exists and signals of elemental hcp Co emerge after 10 discharge/charge cycles. In addition, as shown in ex situ Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS)results, during the 4h discharge/charge cycle, no oxidized Bi signals are observed (Fig. 3(d)), meanwhile the atomic ratio of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}{:}{\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ is high and stable (Fig. 3(e)). These phenomena confirm that K-ion storage is almost entirely from the elemental Bi generated from decomposition of the metastable Bi:Co alloys.
TEM examination was further employed to determine the phase compositions of the $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}@\mathrm{C}$ sample after K-ion uptake/release kinetics becomes stable. Figure S8(a) in the ESM shows TEM image of a $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ sample at its fully charged (i.e., de-potassiated) state after 3 discharge/charge cycles. As shown in the HRTEM image (Fig. S8(b) in the ESM), it finds that the carbon shell demonstrates high flexibility to accommodate the volume expansion/shrink from the Bi potassiation/de-potassiation. Additionally, it can be seen that the cycled $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}\ @\mathrm{C}$ material demonstrates a porous characteristic, similar to the freshly prepared material (i.e., before cycling). A HRTEM image of the charged sample is shown in Fig. S8(c) in the ESM, demonstrating that the precipitated elemental Bi nanoparticles are separated by precipitated Co nanoparticles. Furthermore, as indicated by TEM and HRTEM images of the fully discharge sample (Figs. S8(d) and S8(e) in the ESM), it is found that the K-ion uptake in Bi nanoparticles does not change the porous morphology nor the intimate contact between two types of precipitated nanoparticles, indicating the microstructure stability of samples. The uniform $\mathrm{C},$ Bi, and Co elemental distributions of fully charged $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ sample clearly demonstrate the intimate interactions between precipitated Bi and Co nanoparticles (Fig. S8(f) in the ESM).
Incontrast,in $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ material, repeatedvolume expansion/shrink from potassiation/de-potassiation gives rise to “dead” Bi and $\mathrm{K_{3}B i}$ with electrochemical inactivity after initial several cycles, as shown in ex situ XRD patterns (Fig.S9(a) in the ESM). Furthermore, the repeated volume expansion/shrink also leads to the severe aggregation of adjacent Bi particles, generating large particle with long K-ion diffusion distance (Figs. S9(b)-S9(e) in the ESM). Combining these comparison analysis results of $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}\ @\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ samples, it concludes the precipitated Co nanoparticles can act as“separators” to prevent the adjacent precipitated Bi particles from aggregation and thus to ensure short K-ion diffusion for efficient K-ion uptake/release in each Bi nanoparticles.
On the other hand, benefiting from intimate contact between the Bi and the Co nanoparticles, Bi can effectively react with Kions with the help of Co nanoparticles which act as conductive "binder" for ensuring effcient electron transport. Such enhanced potassiation/de-potassiation kinetics in these metastable materials can be reflected by electrochemical analysis. Figures $4(\mathsf{a}){-}4(\mathsf{c})$ show cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of $\mathrm{Bi}@{\mathrm{C}},$ $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C},$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes after initial potassiation/de-potassiation. With increasing scan rates, the CV curve profiles of $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode change with disappearance of potassiation peak (peak A in Fig.4(a)) due to relative sluggish K-ion storage kinetics. In contrast, changes of CV profiles and shift of K-ion uptake/release peaks are negligible in $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes (Figs. 4(b) and $4(\mathrm{c}))$ . To better understand the differences of kinetics in the three electrodes, their apparent K-ion diffusion coefficients $(D_{\mathrm{K-ion}})$ are determined from the well-observed depotassiation peak (peak B) using the Randles-Sevcik equation [35, 36]
$$
i=2.69\times10^{5}\times A\times n^{2/3}\times D_{\mathrm{K-ion}}^{\mathrm{\phantom{K-}12}}\times C_{0}\times\nu^{1/2}
$$
where, i, $D_{\mathrm{K-ion}},$ and $\nu$ stand for the peak current, the apparent Kion diffusion coeffcient, and the potential scan rate, respectively. $A,n,$ and $C_{0}$ represent the effective surface area of the electrode, the number of electrons transferred in a unit reaction, and the concentration of the diffusion species, respectively. By plotting i vs. $\nu^{1/2}$ (insets of Figs. $4(\mathsf{a})\mathsf{-4}(\mathsf{c}))$ ,the $D_{\mathrm{K-ion}}$ of $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C},$ $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C},$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes are $7.9\times10^{-11}$ 。 $3.1\times10^{-10},$ and $5.6\times$ $10^{-10}~\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{s}^{-1};$ 、 respectively. These again confirm that the alloy electrodes have much faster K-ion diffusion kinetics. The importance of inactive metallic precipitates is also shown from electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). Figures 4(d)-4(f) are EIS spectra of half PIBs employing $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C},$ $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C},$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ anodes respectively after different discharge/charge cycles. Upon cycling, the charge transfer resistance (diameter of the semicircle at low $Z^{\prime}$ ）ofthe $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ increases along with the cycling numbers, gradually increasing from approximately 15 to $60~\Omega$ after 30 cycles (Fig.4(d)). Interestingly, the situations are much improved after alloying with $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ or Fe. In particular, the resistance of the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ anode is stabilized after 10 cycles (Fig.4(f).
Based on above results, the phase and morphology changes of metastable $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{Co}}\ @\mathrm{C}$ structure are illustrated in Fig. $4(\mathrm{g)}$ .The precipitated elemental Co nanoparticles play key roles as “binders' and “separators" to ensure fast and reversible K-ion storage in the precipitated Bi nanoparticles from Bi:Co metastable alloy. Even more attractive is that the performance enhancement is obtained with a significant cost reduction when expensive Bi $(\sim10,300$ USD/ton) is partially replaced with a much cheaper metal such as Fe $(\sim93\mathrm{USD/ton})$
To further investigating application potential of the prepared alloy anodes, full PIBs with a polymer cathode (PTCDA-OC, a polymer with 3,4,9,l0-perylene-tetracarboxylic acid-dianhydride and hydrazine),a pre-potassiated $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ anode, and a $1\mathrm{M}$ KPF/DME electrolyte were assembled. PTCDA-0C is an organic cathode with good rate capability, as demonstrated in our previous works (Fig.Sl0 in the ESM) [37]. Typically, K-ions are inserted into the $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ anode and extracted from the PTCDA-0C cathode during charging in full battery, and vice versa during discharging (Fig. 5(a).
The assembled full cell exhibits excellent electrochemical performance at various current densities (Figs. 5(b) and 5(c)).
Specially, the full cell delivers reversible capacities of 107, 98, 91, and $84\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ at 0.1, 0.2,0.5,and $1\ {\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ 。 respectively. After cycling at high current density, the capacity of the full cell can be restored back to $94{\mathrm{~mAh}}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the current density is reduced back to 0.2 $\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ， demonstrating the superior capacity recoverability. The assembled full PIB can deliver both high energy and power densities (170.18 and $133.6\mathrm{Wh}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at 167 and $1{,}670\ \mathrm{W{\cdot}k g^{-1}}$ 、 respectively). The cycle stability of the full PIB is further investigated (Fig.5(d)). After the first 5 cycles for activation at $0.1\mathrm{\A\cdotg^{-1}}$ ，the assembled PIB exhibits good cycle stability over l00 cycles with a capacity retention of $86\%$ $({80}\mathrm{mAh\cdotg^{-1}})$ at $0.5\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we proposed and fabricated, for the first time, Bi:Fe and Bi:Co metastable alloy nanodots@carbon structures via a facile MOF-assistant annealing method. Comparing with the $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode, the $\operatorname{Bic}\mathrm{co}@\mathrm{C}$ and the Bi:Fe $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}$ electrodes demonstrate significantly improved mass loading-independent rate and cycling performance with high K-ion uptake/release kinetics. Under high current density of $20\mathrm{A\cdotg^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.85}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}@\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{0.83}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.17}@\mathrm{C}$ electrodes respectively deliver superior 178 and $253\mathrm{\mAh{g}^{-1}};$ ，meanwhile only $77\ \mathrm{mAh\cdotg^{-1}}$ is obtained in $\mathrm{Bi}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode. Ex situ SEM,XPS,XRD,and TEM studies on the $\operatorname{Bi}{\mathrm{:Co@C}}$ material indicate that  inactive elemental Co nanoparticles separate out during the potassiation process, acting as efficient electron transport pathways, preventing the adjacent precipitated Bi nanoparticles from aggregation, and accelerating the potassiation/de-potassiation kinetics during the following cycles. The prepared Bi:Co and Bi:Fe alloy nanodots@carbon structures can be promising anode materials for PIBs. Furthermore, the proposed metastable alloy types and material synthesis method could provide new insights on the highperformance metal anodes for batteries.
